<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
        Cleartext is permitted app-wide and the lint warning is suppressed rather than
        fixed, because there is nothing narrower to express: the server is user-supplied
        — an OpenAI-compatible endpoint or an MCP server, often LM Studio or Ollama on
        the same Wi-Fi — and a <domain-config> names literal hosts, not CIDR ranges, so
        no enumeration covers "whatever machine this user runs a model server on".

        Be clear about what that costs, because it is not limited to the LAN and it is
        not opt-in. `normalizeApiUrl` defaults *any* schemaless input to http://, so a
        user who types a public hostname gets a plaintext connection to it without ever
        choosing one. What crosses that connection is the chat itself: message content,
        attached images, voice transcriptions, and `config.token` as a Bearer header.
        The Data Sharing notice covers who receives the data, not whether the transport
        protects it.

        Narrowing this belongs in `normalizeApiUrl` — defaulting to http:// only for
        hosts that are demonstrably local and to https:// otherwise — not here, where
        the only choices are all-or-nothing.
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"
        tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration" />
</network-security-config>
